          "content": "# TOML Configuration Support Design Document - Outline\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n- Feature purpose: Add TOML configuration file support to AWS SDK for Python\n- Key decision: Hybrid session-level implementation with configloader extensions\n- Expected impact: 400-800+ lines across 5-7 files\n- Risk level: High due to data type compatibility challenges\n\n## 2. Problem Statement\n- Current INI format limitations (string-only values, parsing ambiguity)\n- SEP requirements for native TOML data types\n- Customer demand for modern configuration format\n- Backward compatibility requirements\n\n## 3. Design Approach\n### 3.1 Selected Solution\n- Hybrid implementation combining session-level discovery with configloader parsing\n- TOML file precedence over INI files\n- Native data type support with conversion layer\n\n### 3.2 Rejected Alternatives\n- Approach 1: ConfigLoader extension (file path mapping issues)\n- Approach 2: Provider chain extension (architecture mismatch)\n- Approach 3: Pure session-level (excessive caller changes)\n\n## 4. Technical Design\n### 4.1 File Discovery and Precedence\n- `~/.aws/config.toml` default location\n- `AWS_CONFIG_FILE_TOML` environment variable support\n- TOML-first precedence logic in `session.full_config`\n\n### 4.2 TOML Parsing Implementation\n- New functions: `load_toml_config()`, `raw_toml_parse()`\n- Modified `build_profile_map()` for TOML section syntax\n- Native data type handling for boolean, integer, array properties\n\n### 4.3 Data Type Conversion Strategy\n- Leverage existing `ensure_boolean()` where possible\n- Add conversion layer for array properties (`sigv4a_signing_region_set`)\n- Defensive type checking for mixed TOML/INI scenarios\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n### 5.1 File Changes Required\n- `botocore/session.py` - TOML file discovery and precedence\n- `botocore/configloader.py` - TOML parsing functions\n- `botocore/configprovider.py` - New session variable\n- `requirements.txt` - TOML library dependency\n- Additional files for error handling and type conversion\n\n### 5.2 Data Type Mappings\n- Boolean: `true`/`false` → Python `True`/`False`\n- Integer: `3600` → Python `3600`\n- Array: `[\"us-east-1\", \"us-west-2\"]` → Python list\n\n## 6. Risk Assessment and Mitigation\n### 6.1 Critical Risks\n- Array data type incompatibility (no conversion function exists)\n- Multi-file configuration precedence with mixed types\n- Existing test suite compatibility\n- Third-party plugin compatibility\n\n### 6.2 Mitigation Strategies\n- Extensive integration testing\n- Defensive type checking throughout codebase\n- Gradual rollout with feature flags\n- Comprehensive backward compatibility testing\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n### 7.1 SEP Compliance Testing\n- Execute all SEP-provided test cases\n- Validate native data type handling\n- Verify error handling requirements\n\n### 7.2 Integration Testing\n- Mixed TOML/INI configuration scenarios\n- Credentials file merging with type differences\n- Cross-platform compatibility testing\n\n## 8. Deployment Considerations\n### 8.1 Dependency Management\n- Python 3.11+: built-in `tomllib`\n- Python <3.11: external `tomli` dependency\n- Corporate environment compatibility\n\n### 8.2 Rollout Strategy\n- Feature flag for TOML support\n- Gradual enablement across customer base\n- Monitoring for configuration-related issues\n\n## 9. Success Metrics\n- SEP test case pass rate: 100%\n- Existing test suite regression: <5%\n- Performance impact: <20%\n- Customer adoption rate tracking\n\n## 10. Future Considerations\n- Additional TOML features beyond SEP requirements\n- Migration tooling from INI to TOML\n- Cross-SDK consistency maintenance\n\n---\n\n**Document Scope**: Technical implementation details for development team\n**Target Audience**: Senior engineers, technical leads, and architects\n**Estimated Length**: 8-12 pages with code examples and diagrams"
