    "# TOML Configuration Support Design Document - Detailed Outline\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\n- **Feature Purpose**: Implement TOML configuration file support as specified in SEP, providing native data types (boolean, integer, array) as alternative to string-only INI format\n- **Key Decision**: Hybrid session-level implementation combining file discovery in `session.py` with TOML parsing extensions in `configloader.py`\n- **Architecture Impact**: Modifications to configuration loading pipeline without breaking existing provider chain or credential resolution\n- **Risk Level**: High due to fundamental data type compatibility challenges between TOML native types and existing string-based configuration consumers\n\n## 2. Problem Statement\n### 2.1 Current INI Format Limitations\n- All configuration values stored as strings requiring runtime type conversion\n- Boolean ambiguity: `\"true\"`, `\"True\"`, `\"1\"` variations\n- Array values as comma-separated strings prone to whitespace errors\n- No native validation for numeric ranges or data types\n\n### 2.2 SEP Requirements\n- Native TOML data types: boolean (`true`/`false`), integer, array\n- File precedence: TOML over INI when both exist\n- Environment variable: `AWS_CONFIG_FILE_TOML`\n- Section syntax: `[profile.name]`, `[sso-session.name]`, `[services.name]`\n- Strict parsing with no fallback to INI on TOML errors\n\n### 2.3 Backward Compatibility Constraints\n- Existing INI files must continue working unchanged\n- No breaking changes to public APIs\n- Existing environment variables must remain functional\n- Provider chain resolution logic must remain intact\n\n## 3. Design Approach\n### 3.1 Selected Solution: Hybrid Implementation\n- **File Discovery**: Session-level logic in `full_config` property\n- **Parsing**: ConfigLoader extensions for TOML format support\n- **Type Handling**: Conversion layer between TOML native types and existing consumers\n- **Precedence**: TOML file discovery before INI fallback\n\n### 3.2 Rejected Alternatives Analysis\n#### 3.2.1 Approach 1: ConfigLoader Extension Only\n- **Rejection Reason**: Cannot derive TOML file paths from INI file paths\n- **Technical Issue**: `load_config(filename)` API contract prevents internal format switching\n- **Impact**: Would require breaking changes to function signatures\n\n#### 3.2.2 Approach 2: Provider Chain Extension\n- **Rejection Reason**: Provider chain resolves variables, not file contents\n- **Technical Issue**: Session bypasses provider chain for config file loading via direct `configloader.load_config()` calls\n- **Architecture Mismatch**: Config file parsing occurs before provider chain resolution\n\n#### 3.2.3 Approach 3: Pure Session-Level Implementation\n- **Rejection Reason**: Exposes TOML native types directly to all configuration consumers\n- **Impact**: Requires defensive type checking across extensive codebase\n- **Risk**: High probability of breaking existing code expecting string values\n\n## 4. Technical Design\n### 4.1 File Discovery and Precedence Logic\n#### 4.1.1 Default File Locations\n- TOML: `~/.aws/config.toml`\n- INI fallback: `~/.aws/config` (existing)\n- Platform-specific path resolution using existing session logic\n\n#### 4.1.2 Environment Variable Support\n- New variable: `AWS_CONFIG_FILE_TOML`\n- Integration with existing `BOTOCORE_DEFAUT_SESSION_VARIABLES`\n- Precedence over `AWS_CONFIG_FILE` when both set\n\n#### 4.1.3 Session Integration Points\n```python\n# Modified session.full_config property\n@property\ndef full_config(self):\n    if self._config is None:\n        toml_file = self.get_config_variable('config_file_toml')\n        if toml_file and os.path.exists(toml_file):\n            self._config = botocore.configloader.load_toml_config(toml_file)\n        else:\n            # Existing INI logic unchanged\n```\n\n### 4.2 TOML Parsing Implementation\n#### 4.2.1 New ConfigLoader Functions\n- `load_toml_config(config_filename)`: Main entry point matching `load_config()` signature\n- `raw_toml_parse(config_filename)`: TOML file parsing with native types\n- `build_toml_profile_map(parsed_toml_config)`: Section processing for TOML syntax\n\n#### 4.2.2 Section Syntax Handling\n- TOML: `[profile.default]` → profile name extraction\n- Current INI: `[profile default]` → `shlex.split()` parsing\n- **Critical Issue**: Existing `_parse_section()` incompatible with dot notation\n\n#### 4.2.3 Library Integration\n- Python 3.11+: Built-in `tomllib` module\n- Python <3.11: External `tomli` dependency\n- Error handling wrapper for consistent exception types\n\n### 4.3 Data Type Conversion Strategy\n#### 4.3.1 Native Type Mappings\n- **Boolean Properties**: `use_dualstack_endpoint`, `use_fips_endpoint`, etc.\n  - TOML: `true`/`false` → Python `True`/`False`\n  - Existing `ensure_boolean()` handles both string and boolean inputs\n- **Integer Properties**: `duration_seconds`, `metadata_service_timeout`\n  - TOML: `3600` → Python `3600`\n  - Existing code uses `int()` conversion where needed\n- **Array Properties**: `sigv4a_signing_region_set`\n  - TOML: `[\"us-east-1\", \"us-west-2\"]` → Python `[\"us-east-1\", \"us-west-2\"]`\n  - **Critical Issue**: No existing conversion function in configprovider\n\n#### 4.3.2 Conversion Layer Requirements\n- Array-to-string conversion for `sigv4a_signing_region_set`\n- Mixed-type handling in credentials file merging\n- Defensive type checking in `_merge_list_of_dicts()`\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n### 5.1 Core File Modifications\n#### 5.1.1 `botocore/session.py`\n- Modify `full_config` property for TOML file discovery\n- Add environment variable conflict warning logic\n- Preserve existing credentials file merging behavior\n\n#### 5.1.2 `botocore/configloader.py`\n- Add TOML parsing functions with native type support\n- Modify `build_profile_map()` for TOML section syntax compatibility\n- Extend `_parse_section()` or create TOML-specific equivalent\n\n#### 5.1.3 `botocore/configprovider.py`\n- Add `config_file_toml` to `BOTOCORE_DEFAUT_SESSION_VARIABLES`\n- Add array conversion functions for affected properties\n- Extend type conversion infrastructure\n\n#### 5.1.4 Supporting Files\n- `requirements.txt`: TOML library dependency\n- Exception handling extensions for TOML-specific errors\n- Type conversion utilities for mixed TOML/INI scenarios\n\n### 5.2 Data Structure Compatibility\n#### 5.2.1 Output Format Consistency\n- Both TOML and INI produce identical `{'profiles': {...}, 'sso_sessions': {...}, 'services': {...}}` structure\n- Native types preserved where beneficial, converted where necessary\n- Existing code paths receive expected data types\n\n#### 5.2.2 Credentials File Integration\n- TOML config + INI credentials merging\n- Type conflict resolution in profile merging\n- Profile name matching across formats\n\n## 6. Risk Assessment\n### 6.1 Critical Risks\n#### 6.1.1 Array Data Type Incompatibility\n- `sigv4a_signing_region_set` has no conversion function in provider configuration\n- TOML arrays vs INI comma-separated strings create type mismatch\n- Affects authentication and signing logic throughout SDK\n\n#### 6.1.2 TOML Section Syntax Incompatibility\n- Existing `_parse_section()` uses `shlex.split(\"profile name\")` expecting space-separated format\n- TOML `[profile.name]` dot notation requires different parsing logic\n- Fundamental incompatibility with current profile extraction\n\n#### 6.1.3 Multi-File Configuration Precedence\n- `_merge_list_of_dicts()` uses first-wins precedence without type consideration\n- Mixed TOML boolean + INI string creates inconsistent merged configuration\n- Credentials file merging assumes string-based values\n\n#### 6.1.4 Credentials File Merging Type Conflicts\n- `dict.update()` operations assume compatible value types\n- TOML native types + INI strings in same profile create unpredictable results\n- Profile resolution logic may break with mixed data types\n\n### 6.2 High Risks\n#### 6.2.1 Existing Test Suite Compatibility\n- Tests mocking configuration with specific string formats\n- Assertions expecting exact data structures and types\n- Integration tests depending on INI-specific parsing behavior\n\n#### 6.2.2 Third-Party Plugin Compatibility\n- Unknown dependencies on specific configuration data types\n- Custom configuration providers expecting string inputs\n- Extension points accessing raw configuration data\n\n#### 6.2.3 SEP Test Case Compliance\n- All SEP-provided test cases must pass without modification\n- Error handling must match SEP specifications exactly\n- Cross-platform behavior must be consistent\n\n#### 6.2.4 Cross-Platform File System Behavior\n- Case sensitivity differences between operating systems\n- Path separator handling in TOML file discovery\n- File permission models affecting TOML file access\n\n### 6.3 Medium Risks\n#### 6.3.1 Performance Impact\n- TOML parsing overhead compared to INI parsing\n- File existence checking for TOML discovery\n- Memory usage of native data types vs strings\n\n#### 6.3.2 Thread Safety Concerns\n- TOML library thread safety characteristics\n- Concurrent access to mixed-type cached configuration\n- Race conditions in file discovery logic\n\n#### 6.3.3 Unicode and Encoding Handling\n- TOML UTF-8 requirements vs INI ASCII compatibility\n- Byte order mark handling differences\n- Non-English character support in configuration values\n\n---\n\n**Document Scope**: Technical implementation details for development team  \n**Target Audience**: Senior engineers, technical leads, and architects\n[Tool uses: none]",
